\subsection{Graph-cluster randomisation}
\label{sec:cluster}

Cluster designs move the exposures towards the corners, which shrinks the extrapolation and the bias, and reduce the effective sample to about the number of clusters. At $d=20$ the regression under a cluster design has a lower RMSE than the Bernoulli regression in 68 of 78 graph--mechanism--clustering comparisons and than cluster DM in 62 (79\%), both in 54 (69\%), and than a H\'ajek estimator in 68\% of 132 further cells (Appendix~\ref{app:extra}); for the threshold mechanism (d) it beats cluster DM in 2 of 16. Under a Louvain design with about nine clusters on a Barab\'asi--Albert graph, the bias for (d) falls from 0.68 to 0.24 and the SD rises from 0.18 to 0.34. Cluster-bootstrap interval

